# Page template

Build every Civica dashboard view in the same order: a title, badges for anything that needs attention, then sections in order of need. Then a household finds the same things in the same place on every view.

## Keep the Home Assistant frame

Home Assistant draws the frame around every dashboard: the sidebar, the menu button, the view tabs and the edit menu. The Civica theme sets their colours but does not change how they work.

Keep Home Assistant's own navigation. Do not hide the sidebar or the menu button to make a dashboard look tidier, because people need them to get back to their settings.

## Parts of a view

| Part | What goes in it | YAML |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Title, icon and path | The name on the view tab, such as **Home** | `title`, `icon`, `path` |
| Badges | Short states that need attention, such as an open door | `badges` |
| Sections | One group of cards for a room or a task, most urgent first | `sections` |

Start each section with a Heading card that names it, such as **Heating** or **Alerts**. People who use a screen reader can then jump from section to section.

## View template

This YAML is the start of a Home view for an invented household. The badge shows the front door, and the Alerts section comes first.

```yaml
title: Home
path: home
icon: mdi:home
type: sections
max_columns: 4
badges:
  - type: entity
    entity: binary_sensor.front_door
sections:
  - type: grid
    cards:
      - type: heading
        heading: Alerts
      - type: tile
        entity: binary_sensor.kitchen_leak
        name: Kitchen leak
  - type: grid
    cards:
      - type: heading
        heading: Heating
      - type: thermostat
        entity: climate.hallway
```

## Subviews for detail

Use a subview for detail that people open from another view, such as all the lights in one room. A subview has no tab. It shows a back arrow that returns to the view set in `back_path`.

```yaml
title: Kitchen
path: kitchen
subview: true
back_path: /dashboard-home/home
type: sections
```

## Dashboard background

Use a plain background behind controls and readings. A photograph or moving scene behind the whole dashboard makes text harder to read and draws attention from the cards. If a household wants a photograph, put it in a Picture card that has a job, such as showing the garden camera.
